## Findings

### High — The complete two-client graph still lacks exact finalization evidence

The replay/update/FIFO behavior is covered, but its production-shaped graph does not assert exact finalization of both connections, four forwarding fibers, both input processors/queues, listener close/unlink, provider event source, and provider Layer. Separate pre-hello, partial-frame, blocked-sample, and peer-isolation tests provide partial evidence but do not prove the complete graph releases each resource exactly once.

### High — Executable cleanup failure remains unverified at the process boundary

Signal-handler removal and direct Layer composition are covered, but no runtime executable-path test proves signal-driven dependency-order cleanup or that close/unlink failure sets nonzero process status while retaining tagged operation/message diagnostics. Promise-facade and direct-Layer failure tests do not establish this boundary.

### Medium — Blocked-sampling exact cleanup still omits close/unlink and source-acquisition counts

The blocked-sample test now proves socket, listener, input queue/processor, connection, sample interruption, event-source finalization, and provider-Layer finalization. It still does not count listener close/unlink attempts or the provider source acquisition, so exact-once cleanup remains partly inferred from path absence.

### Medium — Actual production closing refusal remains unobserved

Calling `server.close()` at Node callback entry proves the enrolled-and-finalized branch because the callback continues synchronously before the Effect runtime sets `closing`. Synthetic `canEnroll` refusal proves generic destruction, but no callback deterministically observes the real `closing` branch.

### Medium — Post-hello late-forwarder isolation remains conflated with provider teardown

The replay-forwarding test emits only after the entire provider source is disposed and does not await both forwarding-finalizer hooks. Prove that a naturally disconnected post-hello client's forwarders finalize and make no write while the provider, listener, and healthy peer remain live.

### Medium — Older integration tests still lack failure-safe cleanup

Recent lifecycle tests use `try/finally`, but many earlier socket tests release resources only on success paths. An intermediate assertion can leak sockets, listeners, or paths contrary to the package's focused-test cleanup requirement.

